WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.830
Sandstone is dominated by sand-sized grains, commonly quartz but sometimes feldspar,

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.870
volcanic fragments or other minerals.

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:11.550
It may contain some silt or gravel while remaining sandstone.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.510
Grains can be angular or rounded.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.970
Parallel layers, cross beds and massive-looking intervals record different patterns of

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.010
deposition and preservation.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:25.150
Desert dunes commonly produce well-sorted sand and large cross beds.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.630
Iron-oxide cement can give terrestrial sandstone a red color.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:33.270
Streams deposit sand in channels and during floods, commonly interlayered with

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.310
finer floodplain sediment. Use structures and associated deposits together;

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.090
neither red color nor sandstone alone proves desert dunes.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:46.090
Beaches obtain sand from local rocks, reefs, rivers, wind and coastal

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.590
currents. Shells or gravel may accompany it, although energetic waves can

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.170
destroy fragile shells. Wind moves some beach sand into coastal dunes.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:59.310
Deltas also deliver sand through channels and shallow water, so several

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.810
neighboring coastal settings can produce sandstone.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:06.310
The source outcrop changes upward from marine shale with thin sand

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.930
beds to thicker sandstone with less shale.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.770
A delta advanced over the earlier marine mud.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.950
This records a shift toward sandier, more landward conditions at that

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.270
location. Read the whole vertical sequence rather than interpreting the upper

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:21.630
sandstone in isolation.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.650
Sand can remain on a continental shelf or move into deeper

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.190
water. A turbidity current carries a slurry downslope and can leave

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.110
a graded sand bed among quieter marine mud deposits.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.770
Sand near volcanoes may be rich in dark volcanic grains.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.270
Deep-water sandstone is therefore compatible with continental sediment delivered by a

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.050
sudden current.
